I wouldn’t start it on an informal “watch and abort” plan. That’s still **knowingly initiating a clinical run outside the validated limit**, and watching closely doesn’t establish that early-cycle data or materials remain valid. It may also consume or compromise reagents in ways that aren’t reversible—I can’t confirm otherwise without the instrument’s validated procedure.

There is one defensible version of your idea: a **formally authorized conditional start**. Before pressing Start, get Varga **and the designated QA/quality authority** to document:

- that this exact software/frame-drop condition is recognized;
- that starting at 12% is permitted;
- which early-cycle metric proves recovery;
- a numeric checkpoint and deadline for recovery;
- automatic/manual abort criteria;
- how data produced before recovery will be handled;
- whether an aborted start preserves reagent/sample integrity.

Send a concise, non-confrontational message:

> “Ready to launch on your direction. Telemetry remains 12% down versus the validated 8% criterion. Please confirm in the run record and copy QA that this known autofocus condition is approved for a monitored start, with the recovery checkpoint and abort threshold. I’ll monitor continuously and execute those criteria.”

That isn’t stalling; it can take minutes and puts the exception with the people authorized to own it. **If QA cannot authorize a conditional start, don’t improvise one.** If Varga alone has documented authority under the governing SOP, record her instruction and the applicable SOP section—but don’t treat a chat message as overriding validated acceptance criteria.
